  <h2>TL;DR: define the campaign and the handoff before you scale</h2>
  <p>
    An established practice should expect a Google Ads partner to define what is being managed,
    who controls the account, which searches and locations are eligible, where clicks land, which
    conversion actions influence bidding, and how the office will classify inquiries, bookings,
    and attended appointments. ChiroCandy manages Google Ads for chiropractic practices; the exact
    campaign types, pages, tracking, fees, reporting, and practice responsibilities should be
    confirmed in the current proposal and account review.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paid search can make a practice eligible to appear when people search, but it does not promise
    first position, qualified inquiries, appointments, patients, or revenue. The useful buying
    question is whether the campaign, measurement, and office handoff form one reviewable system.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a chiropractic Google Ads engagement define?</h2>
  <p>
    Use the scope below to compare proposals and to turn a sales conversation into named
    responsibilities. A workstream may be unnecessary for a particular practice, but the proposal
    should say whether it is included, excluded, or owned by someone else.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Google Ads scope and review evidence">
    <table>
      <caption>Campaign scope and evidence framework</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Workstream</th>
          <th scope="col">Define before launch</th>
          <th scope="col">Review with evidence</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Account and access</th>
          <td>Business owner, administrators, agency manager access, billing, linked tools, and exit handoff</td>
          <td>Observed access levels, billing owner, linked-account list, and written transition process</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Goal and capacity</th>
          <td>Service, location, accepted inquiry, appointment capacity, decision owner, and spending boundary</td>
          <td>Available appointment path and a shared definition of the business outcome</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Campaign controls</th>
          <td>Campaign type, search themes or keywords, search terms, negatives, locations, schedule, devices, ads, and assets</td>
          <td>Current settings, change history, search-term evidence, and documented exclusions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Landing experience</th>
          <td>Destination pages, message match, phone and form path, booking route, approvals, and page owner</td>
          <td>Mobile and desktop path checks plus an approved non-patient test of the handoff</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Measurement</th>
          <td>Calls, forms, bookings, primary and secondary conversion actions, attribution limits, and consent requirements</td>
          <td>Observed event receipt, goal settings, call or form records, and stated data gaps</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Reporting and decisions</th>
          <td>Media spend, fees, review cadence, inquiry classification, booking reconciliation, and pause authority</td>
          <td>Stage-by-stage totals, denominator definitions, unresolved issues, and a continue, revise, or pause decision</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep account access and ownership explicit</h2>
  <p>
    Ask whether ChiroCandy will link to an existing practice account or whether a different account
    structure is proposed. Google's current <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7456530?hl=en">manager-account guidance</a>
    says linking an existing client account leaves the account and its history intact, and the
    manager does not receive administrative ownership by default. That makes access, ownership,
    and management related but different questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Record who controls billing, administrator access, the website and landing pages, analytics,
    call tracking, conversion imports, and any CRM or scheduler connection. Also record what access
    and export the practice receives if management ends. Do not rely on “you own your data” without
    a list of the accounts, permissions, and transition steps that statement covers.
  </p>

  <h2>Separate media, management, pages, and measurement</h2>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy's current <a href="/pricing/">public pricing page</a> states that advertising spend
    is separate from service fees. A proposal should also identify setup, landing-page work, call
    tracking or other software, conversion configuration, reporting, and practice labor so the
    owner can see the complete commitment. Use the <a href="/blog/google-ads-budget-for-chiropractors/">Google
    Ads test-budget guide</a> to define a spending ceiling and decision boundary without treating a
    generic cost average as a forecast for your market.
  </p>
  <p>
    Confirm who may change budgets and who may pause the campaign if a page breaks, tracking becomes
    unreliable, the location settings are wrong, or the approved spending boundary is at risk.
    Flexible settings are useful only when decision authority is clear.
  </p>

  <h2>Review the searches and locations that actually became eligible</h2>
  <p>
    A keyword is an advertiser setting; a search term is what a person entered. Google's
    <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/13738234?hl=en">search-terms documentation</a>
    explains that the report shows searches that triggered ads and can inform keyword changes and
    negative keywords. Review actual search terms for relevance rather than assuming the keyword
    list describes every query that received an impression or click.
  </p>
  <p>
    Location settings also require review. Google says
    <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/6317?hl=en">location targeting</a> uses
    several signals and is a best effort rather than a guarantee of perfect geographic accuracy.
    Check the selected locations, excluded locations, location options, and observed geographic
    performance against the practice's real service area. The
    <a href="/blog/chiropractic-google-ads-search-terms/">chiropractic search-term review</a>
    provides a focused worksheet for this part of the campaign.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose conversion signals that match the decision</h2>
  <p>
    Google Ads groups measured actions into conversion goals. Its current
    <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/11461796?hl=en">primary and secondary conversion guidance</a>
    says primary actions can appear in the Conversions column and influence bidding when the
    campaign uses the associated goal; secondary actions are generally observed in All conversions
    rather than used for bidding. Custom goals are an important exception, so inspect the actual
    campaign and goal settings instead of relying on the label alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Decide what each action means. A phone connection may include a current patient, vendor, wrong
    number, or new inquiry. A submitted form may be spam or outside the service area. A scheduler
    event may not be an attended appointment. Before changing bidding goals, document which events
    are reliable, which are only diagnostic, and who reviews the downstream records.
  </p>

  <h2>Measure the handoff without collapsing the stages</h2>
  <p>Use a measurement ladder that keeps each denominator visible:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>eligible impression in Google Ads;</li>
    <li>click recorded in Google Ads;</li>
    <li>recorded platform conversion action;</li>
    <li>connected call or received form;</li>
    <li>relevant new-patient inquiry under the practice's definition;</li>
    <li>booked appointment;</li>
    <li>held appointment; and</li>
    <li>accepted patient or financial outcome, only when the practice has approved records and definitions.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Report movement between stages without turning one stage into another. The
    <a href="/blog/chiropractic-call-tracking-lead-quality/">call-quality guide</a> helps classify
    connected calls, and the <a href="/blog/chiropractic-advertising-leads-to-appointments/">lead-to-appointment
    handoff review</a> covers response, booking, and attendance evidence. Platform-reported
    conversions should not be described as patients or revenue unless separately reconciled.
  </p>

  <h2>Make the landing page and office response part of the review</h2>
  <p>
    The destination should clearly match the ad's service and location, explain the next step, and
    make the phone, form, or booking path usable on supported devices. Confirm the live destination,
    tracking, notification recipient, scheduler location and appointment type, privacy disclosures,
    and the practice's response owner before calling the campaign ready.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use an approved non-patient test record where a real submission is necessary. A successful page
    load or button click does not prove that the office received the inquiry or that the booking
    landed in the correct calendar. The <a href="/blog/chiropractic-google-ads-landing-pages/">Google
    Ads landing-page review</a> keeps those checks separate from copy opinions.
  </p>

  <h2>Use the first review to find constraints, not declare victory</h2>
  <p>
    Set a review date and name the evidence required for it. The timing should reflect the approved
    spend, conversion lag, data volume, bidding approach, and appointment capacity—not a universal
    promise that every campaign optimizes on the same schedule. Review earlier when the page,
    tracking, budget, location, or inquiry handoff may be broken.
  </p>
  <p>
    A useful review asks which searches consumed spend, whether the location settings matched the
    service area, whether ads and pages matched the intended service, which conversion actions
    guided bidding, whether recorded inquiries were relevant, and where bookings or attendance
    were lost. End with a documented decision to continue, revise, pause, or run a better-defined
    test. No single platform metric answers every one of those questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Decide whether Google Ads is the right next constraint to fund</h2>
  <p>
    Google Ads may be a reasonable next step when the practice has a defined service and market,
    capacity for additional appointments, a usable landing and booking path, a responsible test
    budget, and staff coverage for inquiries. It may be premature when account access is disputed,
    the website or booking path is broken, conversion signals are unreliable, or the practice
    cannot follow up consistently.
  </p>
  <p>
    If paid search and organic visibility compete for the same budget, use the
    <a href="/blog/google-ads-vs-seo-for-chiropractors/">Google Ads versus SEO guide</a> to compare
    the current constraint. They solve different discovery problems, and neither channel guarantees
    rankings, appointment volume, or a fixed cost per patient.
  </p>

  <h2>Bring the current account and the office workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Bring your current Google Ads access, target market, appointment capacity, landing pages, and
    call-handling process to a <a href="/schedule/">ChiroCandy discovery call</a>. We can identify
    the campaign and measurement questions that need answers before you scale. The goal is a clear
    scope and review plan—not a promise about placement, lead cost, appointments, or revenue.
  </p>
